 ## Background
-The concept of using a differentiable renderer inside an optimizer to estimate pose, geometry, materials, or illumination from images is foundational to inverse rendering [S1, unofficial]. The loop itself is simple: initialize parameters, trace rays, compute loss, backpropagate, and update [S1, unofficial]. However, choosing a faithful model, identifiable parameters, a stable estimator, and a good initialization is difficult [S1, unofficial]. The field has evolved from basic volume rendering optimization [44-radiance-field-optimization.md] to complex stage-based and hybrid approaches [S1, unofficial][S2].
+The concept of embedding a differentiable renderer inside an optimizer to solve inverse problems was formalized in the context of differentiable ray tracing [S1, unofficial]. The loop is conceptually simple: initialize parameters, trace rays, compute loss, backpropagate, and update. However, choosing a faithful model, identifiable parameters, a stable estimator, and a good initialization remains difficult [S1, unofficial]. The field has evolved from basic NeRF training loops to more complex stage-based, staggered, and black-box optimization strategies to handle specific challenges like geometry-material coupling, memory constraints, and non-differentiable renderers [S1, unofficial][S2][S3, unofficial].
 
